---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Layout title="update_all is a SQL escape hatch — it skips your callbacks" description="Why a fast bulk update can leave 50,000 users with broken state." containerClass="max-w-7xl">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/practice/spot-the-tax" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> All lessons
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Spot the Tax · Card 15 of 20</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4"><code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-3xl">update_all</code> is a SQL escape hatch</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">Why a fast bulk update can quietly leave 50,000 users with broken state.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-red-600">❌</span> The code
        </h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">What will this cost you in six months?</p>
        <div class="border-2 border-red-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-red-50/30">
          <CodeBlock code={`class User < ApplicationRecord
  before_save :extend_trial_if_needed

  private

  def extend_trial_if_needed
    self.trial_ends_at = 14.days.from_now if role == "trial"
  end
end

# In a rake task:
User.where(role: "free").update_all(role: "trial")`} language="ruby" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The problem</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">update_all</code> sends a single SQL <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">UPDATE</code> statement directly. It skips callbacks, validations, and even <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">updated_at</code>. That's the whole point of it — it's the fast path. But it also means the <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">before_save</code> that sets <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">trial_ends_at</code> doesn't fire. <strong>50,000 users now have their role set to "trial" but a nil trial_ends_at</strong>, and three days later support starts getting tickets from people whose trial expired the moment they signed up.</p>
      </div>

      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <div id="prompt-section" class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-7">
            <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Take a moment.</strong> Before revealing, think about what you'd use instead when callbacks need to fire — and how you'd make it obvious in the code when callbacks are deliberately being skipped.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pt-2">
            <button
              id="show-solution-btn"
              class="w-full px-6 py-3 bg-ruby text-white rounded-lg hover:bg-ruby-dark transition-colors font-medium"
            >
              Reveal the solution and the principle →
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="solution-section" class="hidden space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-green-600">✅</span> The solution
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">When the model has callbacks that need to fire (or validations you can't skip, or invariants the model is responsible for), <strong>iterate explicitly with <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">find_each</code> and call <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">update!</code> on each record</strong>. It's slower than the SQL escape hatch, but it preserves everything Active Record was doing for you.</p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 space-y-2 ml-4 mb-4">
            <li>Callbacks fire, invariants stay intact</li>
            <li>If you do choose to use <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">update_all</code>, leave a comment that says callbacks are deliberately skipped</li>
            <li>The next reader can tell whether the choice was considered or accidental</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="border-2 border-green-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-green-50/30">
            <CodeBlock code={`User.where(role: "free").find_each do |user|
  user.update!(role: "trial")
end`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>💡</span> The principle at play — Performance shortcuts skip the framework
          </h3>
          <div class="space-y-3 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>Active Record gives you a lot of behavior for free: validations, callbacks, timestamps, dirty tracking, association management. <strong>The price of that is overhead</strong>, and Rails gives you escape hatches like <code class="bg-blue-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">update_all</code>, <code class="bg-blue-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">delete_all</code>, and <code class="bg-blue-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">insert_all</code> for the cases where you don't need any of it and want raw SQL speed.</p>
            <p>The thing to remember is that those escape hatches don't just skip the overhead — <strong>they skip every guarantee the framework was providing</strong>. The validations that protect your data, the callbacks that keep related state in sync, the timestamps that tell you when a row last changed: all of them silently disappear. That's fine when you've thought about it. It's painful when you haven't.</p>
            <p>The discipline is to be deliberate. If you're using a bulk SQL operation, ask "what is this skipping, and am I OK with that?" before you ship it. And leave a one-line comment so the next developer doesn't have to re-derive the answer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/tax-batched-iteration" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 14</a>
      <a href="/lessons/tax-anemic-models" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 16 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <script>
    const btn = document.getElementById('show-solution-btn');
    const prompt = document.getElementById('prompt-section');
    const solution = document.getElementById('solution-section');
    if (btn && prompt && solution) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        prompt.classList.add('hidden');
        solution.classList.remove('hidden');
        if (window.innerWidth < 1024) {
          solution.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
        }
      });
    }
  </script>
</Layout>
